Equivalence Groups Found
└── Kafka producer client for publishing messages to a Kafka topic
    ├── Candidates : kafka, confluent-kafka, aiokafka, pykafka
    ├── Used APIs  : KafkaProducer, producer.send, future.add_callback, future.add_errback, 
    │   producer.flush
    ├── Pitfalls   : confluent-kafka uses a single delivery-report callback passed to 
    │   produce(), not separate future.add_callback/add_errback, and requires explicit 
    │   poll()/flush() calls to trigger callbacks - the success/failure handler split must be 
    │   rewritten; aiokafka is asyncio-native; producer.send returns a coroutine/awaitable 
    │   rather than a future with add_callback/add_errback, requiring the whole 
    │   KafkaClient/KafkaLocust flow to become async; pykafka's Producer API (queue-based, 
    │   linger_ms, sync/async modes) does not expose the same futures-with-callbacks pattern 
    │   and has different error semantics; flush(timeout=...) semantics and exception types 
    │   differ across libraries; value_serializer behavior/signature is kafka-python specific 
    │   and must be re-implemented for other clients
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── kafka-python's KafkaProducer is used here purely as a message-publishing client: 
        constructing with bootstrap_servers/value_serializer, sending messages, attaching 
        success/error callbacks via futures, and flushing on teardown. confluent-kafka, 
        aiokafka, and pykafka all serve the same functional role (Kafka producer), so they are
        legitimate alternatives at the architectural level even though their concrete APIs 
        differ substantially from kafka-python's future-based callback model.

