Equivalence Groups Found
└── green-thread concurrency / monkey-patching for async I/O (used to make Flask-SocketIO work
    with synchronous code)
    ├── Candidates : gevent, eventlet
    ├── Used APIs  : monkey.patch_all
    ├── Pitfalls   : eventlet.monkey_patch() and gevent.monkey.patch_all() patch slightly 
    │   different sets of modules by default (e.g. eventlet patches os, select, socket, 
    │   thread, time, ssl differently) - must verify same modules are patched; Must be called 
    │   at the very top of the entry file before other imports in both cases - order 
    │   sensitive; Flask-SocketIO's async_mode must be explicitly set/matched to whichever 
    │   library is chosen (async_mode='eventlet' vs 'gevent'); Behavioral differences in 
    │   exception propagation and greenlet scheduling between the two libraries can cause 
    │   subtle bugs; trio and twisted use fundamentally different concurrency models 
    │   (structured concurrency / callback-based reactor) with no equivalent monkey-patch 
    │   mechanism, so they are not viable substitutes here
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── gevent.monkey.patch_all() is called at import time to make standard library 
        sockets/threads cooperative, which is required for Flask-SocketIO's synchronous view 
        functions to work with async WebSocket handling. eventlet provides the exact same 
        monkey-patching capability (eventlet.monkey_patch()) and is historically the other 
        officially supported async mode for Flask-SocketIO, making it a genuine drop-in 
        alternative for this specific use case.

